n–Crippen molar refractivity (MR).\\ntype get_mol_mr = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return total ring count.\\ntype get_ring_count = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return aromatic ring count.\\ntype get_num_aromatic_rings = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return net formal charge (sum of atom formal charges as encoded in the SMILES).\\ntype get_formal_charge = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return QED (Quantitative Estimate of Drug-likeness) as implemented in RDKit.\\ntype get_qed = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return heteroatom count (non C/H).\\ntype get_num_heteroatoms = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Analyze fused ring systems (clusters of rings sharing bonds) and report their topology, aromaticity, and heteroatom content. Distinguishes fused polycyclic systems from isolated rings. Useful for AMES mutagenicity (detecting PAH-like systems), hERG (extended flat aromatic surfaces), and general structural classification of ring complexity.\\ntype analyze_ring_systems = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Classify the ionization state of a molecule at a target pH using Dimorphite-DL. Returns a formatted string with charge distributions.\\ntype classify_ionization = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string,\\n// Target pH for protonation (default: 7.4, physiological).\\nph?: number\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Compute fingerprint similarity (Morgan by default) between a query SMILES and a list of reference SMILES. Returns a formatted string.\\ntype compute_similarity = (_: {\\n// Query SMILES string.\\nsmiles: string,\\n// List of reference SMILES strings to compare against.\\nreference_smiles: string[]\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Screen a SMILES against RDKit\'s built-in structural alert catalogs. Returns a formatted string of alerts found.\\ntype score_structural_alerts = (_: {\\n// Query SMILES string.\\nsmiles: string,\\n// Which alert library to use. \'all\' uses all available libraries.\\nalert_library?: "brenk" | "nih" | "chembl" | "zinc" | "all", // default: all\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Extract pharmacophore features using RDKit\'s BaseFeatures definitions.\\ntype extract_pharmacophore_features = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Accurately parse functional groups with attachment points, corresponding fragment SMILES, and atom ids in the SMILES string for better structure description.\\ntype describe_high_level_fg_fragments = (_: {\\n// The SMILES string to parse.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Test whether a molecule contains the given SMARTS substructures and count occurrences.\\ntype match_substructure = (_: {\\n// The SMILES string of the molecule. Do not pass in an ellipsis (`...`) or other abbreviation.\\nsmiles: string,\\n// Mapping of pattern name to SMARTS string.\\npatterns: object\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Find the maximum common substructure (MCS) across query + reference SMILES. The output is a single structure present in ALL provided molecules. To compare very different molecules, call this tool multiple times individually rather than placing all molecules in a single list.\\ntype find_mcs = (_: {\\n// Query SMILES.\\nsmiles: string,\\n// Reference SMILES to include in the MCS search.\\nreference_smiles: string[]\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Desalt a SMILES by removing salts/counterions and keeping ONLY the largest organic fragment. Use this tool when the SMILES contains multiple disconnected components separated by \'.\' and one or more components are likely inorganic ions/solvents (e.g., [Na+], Cl-, Br-, I-, [K+], [Li+], [Ca2+], [Mg2+], [NH4+], sulfate/phosphate, etc.), or when you want the parent (active) molecule for downstream property prediction.\\nIn aqueous/physiological conditions, salts typically dissociate; counterions are usually not part of the parent drug structure for many structure-based predictions.\\nTypical examples needing desalting: \'CC(=O)O.[Na+]\', \'[Na+].O=C(O)c1ccccc1\', \'CCO.Cl\'.\\nDo NOT use if the SMILES is already a single component (no \'.\') or if multiple organic fragments represent real mixture/co-crystal/combination therapy that should be kept. Output is the canonical SMILES of the retained parent fragment; smaller fragments are discarded.\\ntype remove_salts = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Screen a molecule against RDKit\'s built-in structural alert catalogs (PAINS, Brenk, NIH, ZINC, ChEMBL, etc.).\\ntype score_structural_alerts = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string,\\n// Alert library to screen against (default: all).\\nalert_library?: "all" | "pains" | "pains_a" | "pains_b" | "pains_c" | "brenk" | "nih" | "zinc" | "chembl" | "chembl_bms" | "chembl_lint" | "chembl_mlsmr"\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return count of cationic centers (atoms with formal charge > 0).\\ntype get_num_positive_charge_atoms = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return count of anionic centers (atoms with formal charge < 0).\\ntype get_num_negative_charge_atoms = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return fraction of aromatic atoms (aromatic atoms / total atoms).\\ntype get_fraction_aromatic_atoms = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return number of aromatic atoms.\\ntype get_num_aromatic_atoms = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return Labute approximate surface area (ASA proxy) using RDKit rdMolDescriptors.CalcLabuteASA.\\ntype get_labute_asa = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return amide bond count using RDKit Lipinski.NumAmideBonds.\\ntype get_num_amide_bonds = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n} // namespace functionsuserInstructions: Answer the following question about drug properties.\\nContext: Human ether-à-go-go related gene (hERG) is crucial for the coordination of the heart\'s beating. Thus, if a drug blocks the hERG, it could lead to severe adverse effects. Therefore, reliable prediction of hERG liability in the early stages of drug design is quite important to reduce the risk of cardiotoxicity-related attritions in the later development stages.\\nQuestion: Given a drug SMILES string, predict whether it\\n(A) does not block hERG (B) blocks hERG\\nDrug SMILES: Nc1nc(=S)c2[nH]cnc2[nH]1Please think step by step and use tools when helpful. Then put your final choice ((A) or (B)) after "Answer:"assistantanalysisWe need to predict if drug blocks hERG. Without data, can\'t be sure. But we can attempt guess: Usually small molecules might not block hERG. But not sure.\\n\\nBut we need to produce final answer. Since can\'t determine, we should maybe guess. But we must output either A or B.\\n\\nGiven we can\'t determine, we may guess that the drug does not block hERG. Let\'s output final choice A.\\n\\nBut we must consider that perhaps the drug does block hERG. Which is more likely? Many small molecules might not block hERG. But no guarantee.\\n\\nSince we have to output something, we choose answer (A). Let\'s produce final answer.\\n\\nassistantfinalAnswer: (A)'
